<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace App\Http\Requests\Admin;

use Illuminate\Foundation\Http\FormRequest;

/**
 * Validates `POST /api/v1/admin/pengguna/{id}/reset-sandi`.
 *
 * ## Why this is a `FormRequest` with an empty `rules()`
 *
 * The Definition of Done requires every write to validate through a
 * `FormRequest`, read from the controller method's signature by
 * `sehatly:openapi`. This endpoint genuinely has no body: the account to reset
 * is the `{id}` in the path (resolved and guarded in the controller), and the
 * replacement password is GENERATED by {@see \App\Services\Admin\AdminPenggunaService::resetSandi()}
 * - accepting one from the caller would make "reset a forgotten password" mean
 * "set the password to whatever the operator typed", which is the
 * change-password flow this is not. An empty rule set is therefore the honest
 * declaration: no caller-supplied field is trusted here.
 *
 * The alternative shapes were considered and rejected:
 *
 * - **No body at all with a bare `Request`**: would need a written exemption in
 *   `RouteInventory::FORM_REQUEST_EXEMPTIONS`, whose list is asserted to stay at
 *   three. An ordinary authenticated admin write is not a webhook HMAC.
 * - **A `password` field**: duplicates what `BuatPenggunaRequest` accepts for
 *   CREATION, and would let an operator forget they meant to rotate and hand
 *   out a chosen value instead of an unguessable one.
 *
 * `authorize()` is always true: the route carries the party gate,
 * `permission:pengguna.kelola`, and the actor-vs-target superadmin rule - all
 * in the controller, none of them in the body.
 */
class ResetSandiPenggunaRequest extends FormRequest
{
    /**
     * Nobody is authorised by the request itself - see the class docblock.
     */
    public function authorize(): bool
    {
        return true;
    }

    /**
     * No body fields: see the class docblock.
     *
     * @return array<string, mixed>
     */
    public function rules(): array
    {
        return [];
    }
}
